# Guido van Rossum

Guido van Rossum (born January 31, 1956) is a Dutch programmer and computer scientist best known as the creator of the Python programming language. Developed in the late 1980s as a successor to the ABC programming language, Python fundamentally prioritized code readability and expressive syntax, subsequently becoming one of the most widely used programming languages in the world. Van Rossum served as the principal author and lead architect of Python for nearly three decades, guiding its evolution through major structural revisions, including the transitions to Python 2 and Python 3. Within the open-source community, he held the informal title of "Benevolent Dictator For Life" (BDFL), maintaining ultimate decision-making authority over the language's design until he voluntarily stepped down from the role in July 2018. Beyond his foundational work on Python, his career has spanned research and engineering roles at several prominent institutions and technology corporations, including the Centrum Wiskunde & Informatica (CWI), the Corporation for National Research Initiatives (CNRI), Google, Dropbox, and Microsoft. His design philosophy, which emphasizes simplicity and the inclusion of a comprehensive standard library, significantly lowered the barrier to entry for computer programming and catalyzed massive advancements in fields such as data science, artificial intelligence, and web development.

## Early Life and Education

Guido van Rossum was born and raised in Haarlem, the Netherlands. He demonstrated an early interest in electronics and computing, eventually pursuing higher education in the sciences. He attended the University of Amsterdam, where he received a master's degree in mathematics and computer science in 1982. He is the brother of Just van Rossum, a notable type designer and programmer who later collaborated on elements of Python's design and typography.

## Creation of Python

The development of Python originated from van Rossum's experiences working at the Centrum Wiskunde & Informatica (CWI) in the Netherlands during the 1980s.

### Origins and the ABC Language

At CWI, van Rossum spent several years working on the ABC programming language, a teaching language that aimed to be approachable for beginners but lacked the advanced features required by professional systems programmers. In December 1989, seeking a hobby programming project to keep him occupied during the Christmas week, he began writing an interpreter for a new scripting language. His goal was to create a language that inherited the highly readable syntax and user-friendly features of ABC, but included advanced capabilities such as exception handling and interfacing with the Amoeba operating system.

He named the language "Python" not after the reptile, but in honor of the British comedy troupe Monty Python, reflecting his desire to keep the language's culture fun and accessible.

### Design Philosophy and Release

Python was officially published in February 1991 (version 0.9.0). Van Rossum established a core design philosophy emphasizing code readability, utilizing significant whitespace (indentation) to delimit code blocks rather than the curly brackets prevalent in languages like C or Java. Furthermore, he embedded a "batteries included" philosophy into the language, meaning Python shipped with a large, comprehensive standard library capable of performing many common tasks out of the box, preventing programmers from having to write basic functionality from scratch.

Under van Rossum's architectural guidance, the language underwent several major evolutionary phases. Python 2.0, released in 2000, introduced list comprehensions and a garbage collection system capable of collecting reference cycles. Python 3.0, released in 2008, was a major structural revision designed to rectify fundamental design flaws in the language, notably altering how the language handled string encoding by defaulting to Unicode.

## Professional Career

Van Rossum's career trajectory closely followed the growing adoption of Python, transitioning from government research institutions to major Silicon Valley technology corporations.

### CWI, NIST, and CNRI

After working at CWI in the Netherlands until 1995, van Rossum relocated to the United States to work as a guest researcher at the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST). He subsequently joined the Corporation for National Research Initiatives (CNRI) in Reston, Virginia. At CNRI, he spearheaded the "Computer Programming for Everybody" (CP4E) initiative, a DARPA-funded project aimed at democratizing programming literacy and making coding accessible to non-experts.

### Google, Dropbox, and Microsoft

In 2005, van Rossum was hired by Google, where he worked until 2012. Google permitted him to spend half of his working hours continuing the development of Python. During his tenure, he also created Mondrian, an internal web-based code review system utilized heavily by the company, and contributed to the App Engine project.

In January 2013, he joined the cloud storage company Dropbox, which relied heavily on Python for its backend infrastructure and desktop client. He worked at Dropbox until his formal retirement in October 2019. However, finding retirement unfulfilling, van Rossum returned to the technology industry in November 2020, joining Microsoft's Developer Division. At Microsoft, he focused on improving the performance and execution speed of the CPython interpreter.

## Governance and Leadership

From Python's inception in 1989 until 2018, van Rossum operated as the project's "Benevolent Dictator For Life" (BDFL). In open-source software development, this title indicates a founder who retains the final say in disputes or debates regarding the project's direction.

In July 2018, citing the exhaustion of dealing with contentious debates surrounding Python Enhancement Proposal (PEP) 572 (which introduced assignment expressions, colloquially known as the "walrus operator"), van Rossum announced his permanent resignation from the BDFL role. He transferred the governance of the language back to the Python core developers. The community subsequently established a five-member Steering Council to guide the future development of the language in a more decentralized, democratic manner, though van Rossum remained active as a core developer and was initially elected to the council before stepping back.

## Selected Works or Software Projects

* **Python Programming Language** (1991–present) — Original author and lead architect.
* **CPython** — The reference implementation of Python, written in C.
* **Mondrian** — Web-based code review system developed during his tenure at Google.

